  <static seed="49" source="Library/PCC/BasicAlgebra/PointSlopeForm/PointSlopeBasics10.pg">
    <statement><p>A line<rsq/>s equation is given in point-slope form:</p>
<p><m>{y+2}={3\mathopen{}\left(x-1\right)}</m></p>
<p>This line<rsq/>s slope is <fillin characters="5" name="AnSwEr0001"/>.</p>
<p>A point on this line that is apparent from the given equation is <fillin characters="10" name="AnSwEr0002"/>.</p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>3</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\left(1,-2\right)</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>Compare the given equation with a generic point-slope equation:</p>
<p><m>\begin{aligned}
   y-y_{1} \amp = m(x-x_{1}) \\
   {y+2} \amp = {3\mathopen{}\left(x-1\right)}
\end{aligned}</m></p>
<p>We can see the slope is <m>3</m>, and an apparent point on the line <m>(x_{1},y_{1})</m> is <m>{\left(1,-2\right)}</m>.</p>
</solution>
